  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1213 PM CDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nA large cluster of showers and thunderstorms is ongoing across\nsouthern Kentucky as of 11 AM with a few showers and storms\ndipping into West Tennessee. This cluster will continue its E/SE\npropagation through the early afternoon ahead of another round of\nconvection this evening. This cluster has prohibited a warm front\nfrom surging too far north which has allowed temperatures to not\nwarm as rapidly as previous days with temperatures generally in\nthe 80s and approaching 90 degrees. Areas being affected by\nconvection are still much cooler with readings in the 70s. Once\nconvection ends and the warm front clears the area, expect an\ninflux of moisture and heat index values to rise above 105F, and\n110F in some places. The Extreme Heat Warning and Heat Advisory\nwill remain in effect until 8 PM.\n\nA shortwave currently over northeast Kansas and northwest\nMissouri will slowly move towards the Mid-South and threaten\nstrong to severe thunderstorms this evening into the overnight\nhours. Despite this threat being in the short term, details are\nstill not concrete. Each subsequent CAM run delays the arrival of\nthe shortwave into the hours after sunset which decreases surface\nbased instability. HRRR soundings indicate the development of a\nstout cap around 850mb, which would prohibit intense surface\nbased convection. Most nocturnal events this summer have been a\nbust, due to the nocturnal EML, but this event is a bit different\nin that shear will actually be present. The LLJ will ramp up and\nbring a 60 kt maxima across southern Kentucky. The HRRR and NAM\nsuggests this maximum dipping into our northern zones, which\ncould easily threaten damaging winds, despite the CAP. Due to the\nintensity of the LLJ, this also increases helicity at or above\n200 m2/s2. The CAP does bring LCL values a bit higher than\ntypically desired for tornadogenesis, but is very dependent on\nwhere the LLJ sets up. If the LLJ does swing into the northern\nzones, damaging winds and a tornado or two are the primary\nconcerns. Residents are strongly urged to ensure their preferred\nweather alert method is active and audible overnight, given the\nprimary threat occurring during sleeping hours. Rain rates will\nbe intense due to greater than 2\" PW values, but the\nprogressiveness of this system (and days of consecutive dryness)\nshould alleviate major flooding concerns.\n\nThursday during the daytime hours has trended drier on the 12z\nsuite, but there is likely to be remnant outflow boundaries from\nthe overnight storms that could result in diurnally driven\nthunderstorms. Wet microbursts would be the primary concern given\nthe environmental parameters, with a Marginal Risk (level 1/5)\nfor damaging winds generally along and south of I-40. Our\nsouthern zones have one more threatening day for heat, which\ncould be cooler than guidance suggests if convection does\ndevelop, and another Heat Advisory will go into effect at 11 AM\nThursday for heat index values up to 108F.\n\nAfter Thursday, drier air will filter into the region behind a\nweak cold front and could be our first day without heat headlines\nas heat index values are expected to fall below 105F. The weekend\nis also trending cooler and drier under zonal/northwest flow\naloft. There is a chance for showers and thunderstorms on\nSaturday as a departing low over the Great Lakes region stretches\na boundary across much of the southeast, but the column should be\ndry enough to prohibit coverage and accumulation. Long range\nguidance offers two primary scenarios for next week: either\nplacement on the eastern periphery of the ridge (promoting\nnorthwest flow, cooler temperatures, and increased rain chances)\nor direct positioning under the ridge, which would renew hot and\nhumid conditions. We will continue to monitor ensemble trends.\n\nDNM",
